 <!-- Hook -->
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 mb-8">
 <div class="bg-brandBlue/5 border-l-4 border-brandOrange p-6 rounded-r-xl mb-8 speakable">
 <p class="text-lg text-slate-700 italic"><strong>"Both quotes said Qi2. Only one of them could name its authorization IC."</strong></p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mt-4">A German accessory brand shortlisted two Shenzhen factories for a 15W magnetic pad in early 2026. The quotes landed within 8% of each other at MOQ 500. The first supplier returned a coil inductance of 10.0 &micro;H booked against the chip vendor's reference design. The second returned no measurement at all, only a datasheet PDF, and its sample reached 41&deg;C above room temperature after one hour at full load. The brand ordered from the first. The second factory is still quoting.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mt-4">That gap is the whole sourcing problem in one story. China holds the dense end of the world's wireless charging supply chain, and the pad on your desk and the pad that fails at month eight come out of factories that look identical on a directory listing. Per <a href="https://www.grandviewresearch.com/industry-analysis/wireless-charging-market" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-brandBlue hover:text-brandOrange">Grand View Research</a>, the global wireless charging market continues to expand at a double-digit rate, and per WPC industry-recognized standards, magnetic alignment is now the baseline expectation rather than a premium feature. This guide profiles the manufacturers that matter for 2026 and, more usefully, hands you the five coil measurements and one thermal test that separate them.</p>
 </div>
 </div>

