 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">1. How Were These Wireless Charger Manufacturers Selected?</h2>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">Only factories that can evidence WPC certification, measured coil performance and in-house SMT were shortlisted. Wireless charging has no battery to hide behind: a pad that underperforms shows up as heat, not as a quietly degraded capacity figure.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Wireless charging is a smaller supply base than power banks or wall chargers, and it behaves differently. A power bank factory can buy cells on the open market and still ship a defensible product. A wireless charger factory either controls its coil geometry, ferrite sheet and FOD tuning, or it buys a finished module and puts a shell around it. Those two businesses look similar from a directory page and completely different on a test bench.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">We screened on five criteria, in this order:</p>
 <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-600 mb-6">
 <li><strong>WPC membership and per-SKU Qi-ID.</strong> Membership alone is table stakes; the test is whether each quoted model carries an ID you can resolve in the WPC product registry. Factory-wide membership with no per-model registration is a marketing position, not a compliance position.</li>
 <li><strong>Measured coil data.</strong> Inductance, Q-factor, DCR and ferrite permeability are the four parameters that determine whether a 15W pad runs cool or throttles. We require a batch-referenced measurement report naming the instrument.</li>
 <li><strong>In-house SMT and coil winding.</strong> Outsourced coil winding is the single most common source of unit-to-unit variance. If the factory cannot show you the winding line on a video call, assume the coils arrive in boxes.</li>
 <li><strong>Certification support included in the quotation.</strong> CE, RoHS and FCC ID should be quoted as line items, not referred to a third party. This matters most for FCC, where the wireless transmitter needs its own ID rather than a general SDoC.</li>
 <li><strong>Verifiable client references.</strong> At least two, in markets you actually sell into, that you can contact without the factory sitting in the call.</li>
 </ul>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The screening deliberately weights engineering evidence over capacity claims. A 30,000 m&sup2; plant that outsources coils will produce a worse magnetic pad than a 5,000 m&sup2; plant that winds its own, and the difference only appears after the first container lands.</p>
 <div class="bg-brandBlue/5 border-l-4 border-brandOrange rounded-r-xl p-6 mt-6">
 <p class="text-badge font-black text-brandOrange uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">EXPERT INSIGHT</p>
 <blockquote class="text-slate-700 text-base italic leading-relaxed">"The failure mode we see most often is not a bad chip, it is a coil that was wound to the wrong tolerance and never measured. Buyers spend weeks negotiating price and never ask for a single inductance reading. Ask for the number, name the instrument, and half your supplier list disappears."</blockquote>
 <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mt-2">&mdash; Snowy May, Marketing Manager &amp; Founder, WOWOHCOOL</p>
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
</section>

