<section id="choosing" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">4. How to Choose the Right Wireless Charger Supplier?</h2>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">Confirm the coil spec first, the MOQ tier second and the price last. Inverting that order is why brands end up renegotiating in week six.</p>
 <div class="space-y-6 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
 <div>
 <h3 class="text-lg font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">1. Fix the magnetic standard before you fix the form factor</h3>
 <p>Decide whether you are shipping Qi2 MPP at 15W or moving to the Qi2.2 25W extended profile. The two carry different certification work and different coil tuning, and the decision changes what your supplier has to quote. A pad designed for 15W does not become a 25W pad with a firmware flag; the magnetic array and thermal path change with it. If your roadmap includes 25W, tell the factory on the first call rather than after the enclosure is tooled. Our <a href="/blog/wireless-charging-works/" class="text-brandOrange hover:underline">Qi2 magnetic alignment explanation</a> covers the coupling physics if you need the background before that call.</p>
 </div>
 <div>
 <h3 class="text-lg font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">2. Ask who owns the design, then ask for MOQ</h3>
 <p>MOQ is a consequence of design ownership, not a knob the sales team turns. If the factory's platform design stays as-is and you add a laser-engraved logo, you are in the 500-unit tier with 3-7 day samples. If you want custom packaging, the tier moves to 2,000 units. If you bring your own drawing that needs a new injection mold, the tier is 3,000 units plus two to three months of tooling. Getting this wrong is the most expensive mistake in the process: a brand that plans a 500-unit test run against a custom-enclosure design will discover the mismatch after tooling has been quoted, not before.</p>
 </div>
 <div>
 <h3 class="text-lg font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">3. Do the Qi2 cost math so a cheap quote cannot fool you</h3>
 <p>An officially certified Qi2 product carries a WPC-authorized authentication IC costing 0.80-1.50 USD plus a per-unit royalty. Total certification adder lands at 1.20-2.00 USD per unit against a non-certified Qi-compatible build. That number is your sanity check: if a quote undercuts a certified competitor by more than about 2 USD at the same power and form factor, the supplier is almost certainly shipping non-certified hardware that cannot display the Qi2 mark. Non-certified pads are 1.00-1.50 USD cheaper and are legal to sell, but they cannot use the trademark, and retail platforms increasingly filter on it. Our <a href="/blog/qi-certification-guide/" class="text-brandOrange hover:underline">full WPC certification process and cost breakdown</a> walks through the registry checks.</p>
 </div>
 <div>
 <h3 class="text-lg font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">4. Run one thermal test yourself before releasing tooling</h3>
 <p>Order 3-5 units from the top two candidates and run each at 15W continuous for one hour in a 25 &deg;C room. Log the case temperature rise. A well-tuned pad settles near 17.5 &deg;C above ambient; a pad that climbs past 30 &deg;C is losing energy in the coil or the ferrite rather than the silicon, and it will throttle on the customer's nightstand during the second year of the warranty. System efficiency at 15W should land between 82% and 85% DC-in to DC-out, with output ripple under 25 mVpp on the 5V rail. If a supplier cannot reproduce those numbers on demand, the specification was never real.</p>
 <div class="bg-brandBlue/5 border-l-4 border-brandOrange p-4 rounded-r-xl mt-4">
 <p class="text-slate-700 text-sm italic">"Marcus, an Austrian furniture brand, approved a 4,000-unit bedside charger based on a catalog spec sheet. The first container ran 34 &deg;C above ambient at 15W and throttled to 7.5W within 40 minutes. The retune cost three weeks and a new ferrite supplier. The coil had never been measured against the ferrite it was paired with."</p>
 </div>
 </div>
 <div>
 <h3 class="text-lg font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">5. Lock the QC regime into the purchase contract</h3>
 <p>Specify the inspection standard rather than accepting "strict QC". A defensible regime is four-stage inspection covering incoming components, in-process assembly, final function and outgoing packaging, with AQL 0.65 on normal and 0.25 on tightened inspection, and a 100% four-hour aging test on every unit rather than batch sampling. Ask what the factory's production yield and defect rate are and compare against the industry's 95-97% yield and 2-5% defect norm. Third-party inspection runs 300-800 USD per visit and is worth scheduling for the first production order, not the third.</p>
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
</section>

<section id="certification" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">5. Qi2 Certification: Evidence Buyers Must Verify</h2>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">Five coil measurements and one WPC registry lookup verify more about a wireless charger supplier than any certificate pack. Ask for all six before you release tooling.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">Certificates are easy to forward and hard to fake convincingly only if you check the registry. The five parameters below come from an actual production QC run of 50 units, not from a reference board, which is exactly why they are worth asking for. Any factory that cannot produce an equivalent batch report is telling you it does not measure its own output.</p>
 <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
 <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
 <thead>
 <tr class="bg-brandBlue text-white text-badge font-black uppercase tracking-widest">
 <th class="p-4 text-left">Parameter</th>
 <th class="p-4 text-left">Target Value</th>
 <th class="p-4 text-left">How It Is Measured</th>
 </tr>
 </thead>
 <tbody class="text-slate-600">
 <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
 <td class="p-4 font-bold text-brandBlue">Tx Coil Inductance (Ls)</td>
 <td class="p-4">10.0 &micro;H &plusmn;5% @ 100 kHz</td>
 <td class="p-4">LCR meter, e.g. Keysight E4980A</td>
 </tr>
 <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
 <td class="p-4 font-bold text-brandBlue">Coil Q-Factor</td>
 <td class="p-4">&gt; 80 @ 100 kHz, 1 Vrms</td>
 <td class="p-4">Same LCR meter, Q mode</td>
 </tr>
 <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
 <td class="p-4 font-bold text-brandBlue">DC Resistance (DCR)</td>
 <td class="p-4">95 m&Omega; &plusmn;8%</td>
 <td class="p-4">Four-wire Kelvin measurement</td>
 </tr>
 <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
 <td class="p-4 font-bold text-brandBlue">Ferrite Shield &mu;i</td>
 <td class="p-4">2,300 (MnZn, PC95 class)</td>
 <td class="p-4"><cite>IEC 62044-2</cite> test method</td>
 </tr>
 <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
 <td class="p-4 font-bold text-brandBlue">FOD Response Time</td>
 <td class="p-4">&lt; 180 ms</td>
 <td class="p-4">Q-factor deviation method, <cite>WPC PT 1450</cite></td>
 </tr>
 <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
 <td class="p-4 font-bold text-brandBlue">Thermal, 15W continuous 1h</td>
 <td class="p-4">+17.5 &deg;C over 25 &deg;C ambient</td>
 <td class="p-4">Thermal camera, e.g. FLIR E8</td>
 </tr>
 <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
 <td class="p-4 font-bold text-brandBlue">System Efficiency @ 15W</td>
 <td class="p-4">82-85% DC-in to DC-out</td>
 <td class="p-4">Electronic DC load plus oscilloscope</td>
 </tr>
 <tr>
 <td class="p-4 font-bold text-brandBlue">Output Ripple (5V rail)</td>
 <td class="p-4">&lt; 25 mVpp @ 15W load</td>
 <td class="p-4">Oscilloscope, 20 MHz bandwidth limit</td>
 </tr>
 </tbody>
 </table>
 </div>
 <p class="text-xs text-slate-400 mt-2">*Values are mean averages from a 50-unit production sample. Any supplier quoting these parameters must also name the instrument and the batch.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4 mt-6">Certification cost and timing sit alongside the measurements, and both are repeatedly underestimated. WPC lab testing runs 3,000-5,000 USD per SKU over 4-8 weeks, and WPC membership runs 5,000-25,000 USD per year depending on tier. The FCC ID for a wireless transmitter is a separate 2,000-5,000 USD and 3-6 weeks from the general SDoC that covers a wired charger. Budget the certification line before you negotiate unit price, because it is a fixed cost that does not scale down with volume.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Finally, verify rather than trust. Every Qi2 product carries a Qi-ID that resolves in the WPC product registry. Look up the ID for the exact model you are buying, not the model family, and confirm the manufacturer name on the registry entry matches the company issuing your quotation. A trading company quoting a factory's certification is the most common mismatch, and it is a five-minute check. For the wider picture on detecting forged documents, see <a href="/blog/verify-fake-charger-certificates-fcc-ce-ul/" class="text-brandOrange hover:underline">how to verify FCC, UL and CE certificates</a>.</p>
 <div class="mt-6 mb-6">
 <img src="/image/blog/how-to-choose-factory/wireless-charger-internal-coil-structure-fod.webp" srcset="/image/blog/how-to-choose-factory/wireless-charger-internal-coil-structure-fod-450w.webp 450w, /image/blog/how-to-choose-factory/wireless-charger-internal-coil-structure-fod.webp 800w" sizes="(min-width: 1440px) 848px, (min-width: 1280px) 848px, (min-width: 1024px) 848px, (min-width: 768px) 720px, (min-width: 430px) 382px, 327px" alt="Internal Qi2 wireless charger coil structure and ferrite shield for foreign object detection testing" width="800" height="600" loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="max-w-3xl mx-auto rounded-2xl shadow-lg w-full">
 </div>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The image above shows the stack that the five measurements describe: transmit coil, MnZn ferrite shield and the PCBA carrying the foreign object detection circuit. FOD is the safety gate that stops a pad from cooking a set of car keys, and it is the one parameter where a cheap design fails visibly. Per WPC industry-recognized standards, response time under 180 ms is the working target; pads that take longer are typically running a simplified detection routine with a narrower thermal window.</p>
 </div>
 </div>
</section>

<section id="regions" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">6. Which Manufacturing Hub Is Best for Wireless Chargers?</h2>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">Shenzhen for magnetic and IC depth, Dongguan for mid-volume cost balance, Guangzhou for the lowest unit cost at high volume. The right answer depends on your order profile, not on a ranking.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Wireless charging is less geographically distributed than battery assembly. The magnetic stack depends on ferrite sheet suppliers, coil winding houses and Qi2 authentication IC distribution, and those three sit within a short radius of each other in the Pearl River Delta. That concentration is the reason a Shenzhen factory can quote a coil change in days while a factory two provinces away quotes it in weeks.</p>
 <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
 <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
 <thead>
 <tr class="bg-brandBlue text-white text-badge font-black uppercase tracking-widest">
 <th class="p-4 text-left">Hub</th>
 <th class="p-4 text-left">Strength</th>
 <th class="p-4 text-left">Trade-off</th>
 </tr>
 </thead>
 <tbody class="text-slate-600">
 <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
 <td class="p-4 font-bold text-brandBlue">Shenzhen</td>
 <td class="p-4">Coil, ferrite and Qi2 IC sourcing density; fastest NPI and engineering response</td>
 <td class="p-4">Highest labour and overhead cost; quotes run above Dongguan</td>
 </tr>
 <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
 <td class="p-4 font-bold text-brandBlue">Dongguan</td>
 <td class="p-4">Balanced cost and quality; strong mid-volume accessory capacity</td>
 <td class="p-4">Qi2 engineering depth varies plant by plant; validate per SKU</td>
 </tr>
 <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
 <td class="p-4 font-bold text-brandBlue">Guangzhou</td>
 <td class="p-4">Lowest unit cost at high volume; large multi-category plants</td>
 <td class="p-4">Longest engineering queue; weaker fit for small or frequent runs</td>
 </tr>
 <tr>
 <td class="p-4 font-bold text-brandBlue">Foshan / Zhongshan</td>
 <td class="p-4">Furniture and appliance integration; legacy lighting and hardware supply chain</td>
 <td class="p-4">Few standalone charger factories; usually a sub-supplier relationship</td>
 </tr>
 </tbody>
 </table>
 </div>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">A practical pattern many brands settle into: keep the design and first production run with a Shenzhen partner that can iterate on coil tuning quickly, then move repeat orders of a frozen SKU to a Dongguan line for the cost delta. That works because the SKU is fixed by then, but it only works if the receiving factory can reproduce the same measurement report. Write the coil parameters into the transfer package before the second supplier is onboarded, otherwise the second run will drift and you will not know why.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Worth stating plainly: a factory's location predicts its cost structure and its engineering queue, not its quality. The five measurements in section 5 are location-independent, and a Dongguan plant that can produce them is a better partner than a Shenzhen plant that cannot.</p>
 <div class="mt-6 mb-6">
 <img src="/image/blog/oem-vs-odm-guide/oem-odm-wireless-charging-qc-testing.webp" srcset="/image/blog/oem-vs-odm-guide/oem-odm-wireless-charging-qc-testing-450w.webp 450w, /image/blog/oem-vs-odm-guide/oem-odm-wireless-charging-qc-testing.webp 800w" sizes="(min-width: 1440px) 848px, (min-width: 1280px) 848px, (min-width: 1024px) 848px, (min-width: 768px) 720px, (min-width: 430px) 382px, 327px" alt="Wireless charging QC testing station verifying Qi2 output power at a China OEM factory" width="800" height="600" loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="max-w-3xl mx-auto rounded-2xl shadow-lg w-full">
 </div>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Once the supply base is settled, the commercial model question follows. If you are still deciding between using the factory's platform design and bringing your own, the trade-offs are covered in our <a href="/blog/oem-vs-odm-guide/" class="text-brandOrange hover:underline">OEM vs ODM decision guide</a>. If you want to benchmark wireless charging against a category with a longer production history and more mature supplier scrutiny, see <a href="/blog/top-power-bank-manufacturers-china/" class="text-brandOrange hover:underline">our power bank factory comparison</a>, which profiles the same supplier landscape from the battery side.</p>
 </div>
 </div>
</section>

 <!-- Conclusion -->
<section id="conclusion" class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 mb-16">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">Sourcing Wireless Chargers Without the Guesswork</h2>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The wireless charger supply base in China is smaller and more technically gated than the battery category, which is good news for buyers who do the work. Two suppliers quoting the same MOQ and the same wattage are not quoting the same product. The difference is a coil that measures 10.0 &micro;H on a named instrument and one that does not, and it takes one measurement report and one thermal test to tell them apart.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Work in this order: fix the magnetic standard, confirm who owns the design so the MOQ tier is real, do the Qi2 cost math so a suspicious quote is obvious, run the one-hour load test yourself, and write the QC regime into the contract. Any supplier that resists any step of that sequence is giving you information.</p>
 </div>
 </section>

